content.description:
`VaultInfo::try_from` in `programs/vault/src/loaders/vault_loaders.rs` carries two comments — "owned by vault program" and "has discriminant" — describing checks that were never implemented. The body is simply `Self { info: info.clone() }.validate()`, and `VaultInfo::get` performs a bare `bytemuck::from_bytes::<Vault>(&data[0..size_of::<Vault>()])`, reinterpreting whatever bytes the supplied account happens to hold. `Vault::validate` then only enforces `require_ne!(self.assets_mint, self.shares_mint)`, a self-consistency check over those same attacker-chosen bytes.

Because `VaultInfo::try_from` is the single gate through which every context (`DepositContext`, `DepositWithFeeContext`, `RedeemSharesContext`, `UpdateRewardContext`, `SlashContext`, `CollectFeeContext`) turns the vault account into typed state, the entire `Vault` struct becomes attacker-chosen whenever a look-alike account is passed.

For fee collection, the downstream checks in `CollectFeeContext::validate` are:

```rust
require_eq!(&vault.assets_mint,          self.assets_mint.key,          ...);
require_eq!(&vault.vault_assets_account, self.vault_assets_account.key, ...);
require_eq!(&vault.admin,                self.authority.as_ref().key,   ...);
```

Every left-hand side is read out of the unowned account, so all three comparisons are self-referential and vacuous: choosing `admin` = the signing authority, `assets_mint` = the passed mint and `vault_assets_account` = the passed source account satisfies them trivially. The handler then clears the fee counter and calls `spl_transfer_assets_from_vault` with `effect.assets_to_user`, signing with seeds `[b"assets", vault_info.key, vault.vault_assets_account_bump]` — where the bump used to produce the PDA signature is *also* taken from the attacker-chosen bytes.

The rule `rule_collect_fee_requires_program_owned_vault` assumed only that `accounts[0].data_len() == size_of::<Vault>()`, that `accounts[0].owner != &crate::ID`, and that the recorded `fee_amount > 0`, then drove `process_collect_fee` with `.unwrap()` so only accepted executions survive, asserting `transfers == 0`. The counterexample walks straight through `CollectFeeContext::load` → `CollectFeeContext::validate`, both returning normally, and records `fee_before: 1`, `transfers: 1`, `paid: 1`, `fee_after: 0` — the handler accepted an account the program does not own as authoritative vault state and issued a vault-signed payout against it.

The rule `rule_update_reward_rejects_foreign_owned_vault` assumes only that the two accounts are well-shaped (`a[0].data_len() == size_of::<Vault>()`, `a[1].data_len() == spl_token::state::Account::LEN`), that their keys are distinct, that the asset account is genuinely owned by the SPL Token program, and that the vault state account is owned by some program other than this one (`*a[0].owner != crate::ID`). Under those assumptions it asserts `process_update_reward` must return `Err`. The Prover refuted it.

In the counterexample, `vault_info owner` differs from the vault program id, so the account in slot 0 is genuinely foreign-owned, yet the trace continues past `UpdateRewardContext::load` without an error, proceeds into `spl_token_account_amount` → `<spl_token::state::Account as Pack>::unpack_from_slice`, and finishes with `r.is_err(): '0'`. The vault state visibly moved: `v0.num_assets(): 9298` became `v1.num_assets(): 0x300000019` (12,884,901,913) while `num_shares` stayed at 3806 — i.e. the handler computed `reward = decoded_balance - 9298` from attacker-supplied bytes and wrote the result back.

Root cause, entirely in the program's own code:

```rust
fn try_from(info: &AccountInfo<'info>) -> Result<Self, Self::Error> {
    // owned by vault program
    // has discriminant
    Self { info: info.clone() }.validate()
}
```

Both comments describe checks that do not exist. `validate()` reduces to `Vault::validate`, which is the single guard `require_ne!(self.assets_mint, self.shares_mint, VaultError::GuardFail)`. There is no comparison of `info.owner` against `crate::ID`, no discriminator byte is read, and no length bound precedes `bytemuck::from_bytes::<Vault>(&data[0..size_of::<Vault>()])`. Whatever bytes the caller supplies are reinterpreted as a `Vault`.

The one additional guard in `UpdateRewardContext::validate` — `require_eq!(&vault.vault_assets_account, self.vault_assets_account.key, ...)` — is self-referential and therefore vacuous: `vault.vault_assets_account` is read out of the same attacker-controlled bytes, so the attacker chooses both sides of the equality by writing into the forged vault the key of whichever account they pass in slot 1. Nothing further on the path (`process_update_reward` → `spl_token_account_amount` → `vault_update_reward` → `Vault::add_token`) ever consults `vault_info.owner`.

Scope note on persisted damage: on a live validator the runtime forbids a program from mutating data of an account it does not own, so the *write-back* in this particular trace would be rejected at transaction end. What is not blunted is the read side — which account is treated as the vault's asset account, what balance is recognized, and what arithmetic is performed are all decided from forged data before the runtime intervenes. More importantly, the defect lives in the shared loader, so the same missing owner check gates `DepositContext`, `RedeemSharesContext`, `SlashContext` and the fee contexts, which do mutate accounts the program legitimately owns.

The Certora Prover refuted `rule_deposit_not_dead_when_shares_zero`, which asserts that from a vault with `shares == 0` and `assets > 0`, a strictly positive, non-overflowing deposit must still be accepted. The counterexample is fully concrete: `s0 = 0`, `a0 = 1`, `amount = 2^64 - 2` (so `a0 + amount = 2^64 - 1`, satisfying the no-overflow assumption), and `r.is_ok() = 0` — `vault_deposit_assets` returned `Err`.

The chain is fully determined by the source. `vault_deposit_assets` (programs/vault/src/operations/vault_operations.rs) starts with:

```rust
let shares_to_user = vault.convert_assets_to_shares(tkn_amt)?;
vault.mint_shares(shares_to_user)?;
vault.add_token(tkn_amt)?;
```

`Vault::convert_assets_to_shares` (programs/vault/src/state.rs) selects the bootstrap path by comparing the two totals:

```rust
let shares = if self.num_shares() == self.num_assets() {
    token
} else {
    mul_div_floor(token, self.num_shares(), self.num_assets())?
};
```

With `shares = 0` and `assets = 1` the totals differ, so the bootstrap branch is skipped and the ratio branch runs: `mul_div_floor(amount, 0, 1)` multiplies by the zero share total and returns `Ok(0)`. `mint_shares` then hits `require_gt!(amt, 0, VaultError::GuardFail)`, returns `Err(VaultError::GuardFail)` before mutating any state, and `?` propagates the error out of `vault_deposit_assets`; `add_token` is never reached.

Crucially, the annihilation by the zero share total happens for *every* positive `tkn_amt` — the solver simply chose the largest amount permitted by the overflow assumption; `amount = 1` yields the identical `Err`. This makes the state absorbing rather than a transient quirk: once `shares == 0 && assets > 0`, the implied share price is `0 / assets` forever, every deposit mints zero shares and reverts, and no new shares can ever be created to unstick the vault.

The design flaw is that the bootstrap case is keyed on `shares == assets` (which happens to cover a brand-new vault, `0 == 0`) rather than on an empty share supply. The property text names two routes into the bad state: (a) donating tokens to the vault's asset account followed by the permissionless `process_update_reward`, which raises `vault.assets` without touching `vault.shares`; and (b) full redemption of all shares after reward accrual, where `del_token` removes only the floor-rounded asset amount and leaves a residue behind. In either case the residual assets have no outstanding shares to redeem against and are stranded permanently.

Because all deposit entry points (`vault_deposit_assets`, `vault_deposit_assets_exact`, `vault_deposit_assets_with_fee`, `vault_deposit_assets_with_fee_exact`) price through the same `convert_assets_to_shares` helper, the defect is shared by the whole deposit surface; this particular counterexample, however, only drives and therefore only demonstrates `vault_deposit_assets`. Separately, `convert_shares_to_assets` and `convert_shares_to_assets_ceil` use the same `shares == assets` test and divide by `num_shares()`, so a zero share supply would reach a zero divisor there — a lead worth checking on the redemption path, not something this trace establishes.

Remediation should key the bootstrap on an empty share supply (`self.num_shares() == 0 || self.num_shares() == self.num_assets()`), applied consistently across all four conversion helpers. Note that a bare 1:1 bootstrap in a vault that already holds assets hands the residual `A` to the first depositor — the classic ERC-4626 donation/inflation hazard, which this codebase's "exact" deposit variants are explicitly meant to mitigate. Virtual share/asset offsets, a permanently-locked minimum share minted at initialization so the supply can never return to zero, or an admin-only sweep that zeroes `assets` while `shares == 0` (restoring `shares == assets == 0`) are more coherent fixes. A purely defensive variant — having `process_update_reward` refuse to raise `assets` while `shares == 0` — blocks the donation route but neither repairs an already-bricked vault nor covers the full-redemption-with-residue route.

The property `standard_deposit_must_not_bypass_configured_fee` requires that when a vault is configured with a non-zero `fee_bps`, the fee-free deposit variants (`process_deposit` / `process_deposit_exact`, backed by `vault_deposit_assets` / `vault_deposit_assets_exact`) must reject the deposit, forcing depositors through the fee-charging instructions.

`rule_no_fee_bypass_deposit` drives `crate::operations::vault_deposit_assets` directly on a nondeterministic vault constrained to a validly configured, non-zero fee (`fee_bps != 0 && fee_bps <= 10_000`) plus conditions that make the deposit arithmetically viable (`s0 > 0`, `a0 > 0`, `s0 <= a0`, `amount > 0`, no `checked_add` overflow), and asserts the call returns an error. The assertion fails on a fully concrete witness:

- `fee_bps = 10000` (non-zero and exactly `ONE_IN_BPS`, the maximum `FeeBps::try_from` accepts — a legitimately configured vault)
- `s0 = 1`, `a0 = 1` (solvent, `shares == assets` identity branch)
- `amount = 0xfffffffffffff07b` (positive, no overflow)
- `r.is_err() = 0` — the operation returned `Ok`

The root cause is directly visible in the source. In `programs/vault/src/operations/vault_operations.rs`, the guard exists only as commented-out code:

```rust
pub fn vault_deposit_assets(vault: &mut Vault, tkn_amt: u64) -> VaultResult<VaultEffect> {
    //let fee = vault.fee_in_bps()?;
    //require!(fee.is_zero(), crate::errors::VaultError::GuardFail);
    let shares_to_user = vault.convert_assets_to_shares(tkn_amt)?;
    vault.mint_shares(shares_to_user)?;
    vault.add_token(tkn_amt)?;
    Ok(VaultEffect { shares_to_user, assets_to_vault: tkn_amt, ..Default::default() })
}
```

With those lines disabled, nothing on this path reads `vault.fee_bps` at all: `convert_assets_to_shares` takes the identity branch, `mint_shares` and `add_token` succeed, and the function returns `Ok` with `vault.fee_amount` never incremented and nothing routed to the fee token account. The configured fee of 10000 bps plays no part in the computation. No other layer compensates — `Vault::validate` only checks `assets_mint != shares_mint`, and `DepositContext::validate` only checks recorded keys and PDA re-derivations; neither reads `fee_bps`.

The magnitude of `amount` in the witness is incidental: with `s0 == a0` any positive amount reproduces the same `Ok`. By source inspection, `vault_deposit_assets_exact` contains no `fee_bps` reference whatsoever (not even commented out), so the same bypass is expected on the exact-deposit variant, though this counterexample only establishes it for `vault_deposit_assets`.

Because `process_deposit` calls this operation unconditionally and `?`-propagates its result, the bypass is reachable from the public instruction surface: the fee is enforced solely by the caller's choice of instruction, which is to say not enforced at all.

The vault exposes two classes of deposit paths: fee-charging ones (`process_deposit_with_fee` / `process_deposit_with_fee_exact`) and "fee-free" ones (`process_deposit` / `process_deposit_exact`). The fee-free variants are only safe to expose while `vault.fee_bps == 0`; the intended protection is the `require!(fee.is_zero(), VaultError::GuardFail)` check, which is present but **commented out** in `vault_deposit_assets` (`programs/vault/src/operations/vault_operations.rs`) and was never written at all in the `_exact` variant.

`vault_deposit_assets_exact` in `programs/vault/src/operations/vault_exact_deposit_operations.rs` is, in full:

```rust
pub fn vault_deposit_assets_exact(vault: &mut Vault, tkn_amt: u64) -> VaultResult<VaultEffect> {
    let shares_to_user = vault.convert_assets_to_shares(tkn_amt)?;
    let assets_to_vault = vault.convert_shares_to_assets(shares_to_user)?;
    vault.mint_shares(shares_to_user)?;
    vault.add_token(assets_to_vault)?;
    Ok(VaultEffect { shares_to_user, assets_to_vault, ..Default::default() })
}
```

There is no reference to `fee_bps`, no call to `fee_in_bps()`, and no guard. The conversion helpers read only `num_shares()`/`num_assets()`, and `mint_shares`/`add_token` touch only the totals they modify. The configured fee rate is structurally invisible to this code path.

`rule_no_fee_bypass_deposit_exact` formalizes property `standard_deposit_must_not_bypass_configured_fee`: given a validly configured non-zero fee (`fee_bps != 0 && fee_bps <= 10_000`) and a vault state in which a deposit would otherwise plainly succeed, the fee-free operation must return an error. The Prover refuted it with a concrete, fully-concretized witness: `fee_bps = 4` (0.04%, an ordinary in-range fee), `s0 = 8`, `a0 = 14` (solvent vault), `amount = 5`, and `r.is_err() = 0` — the fee-free operation returned `Ok`. Following the trace: `convert_assets_to_shares(5) = floor(5*8/14) = 2`, `convert_shares_to_assets(2) = floor(2*14/8) = 3`, `mint_shares(2)` and `add_token(3)` both pass their positivity and `checked_add` guards, and the function returns `Ok(VaultEffect { shares_to_user: 2, assets_to_vault: 3, .. })`. `vault.fee_amount` is never incremented and nothing is routed toward the fee token account.

The rule's subject is the operations layer, so the counterexample directly proves the accounting core accepts a fee-free deposit on a fee-bearing vault. That `process_deposit_exact` inherits the bypass follows from source reading rather than from this trace: the handler calls `vault_deposit_assets_exact` unconditionally and `?`-propagates the result, and no other reader of `fee_bps` sits between `DepositContext::load` and the two token CPIs.

The net effect is that the fee is "enforced" only by the caller's choice of instruction — which is to say, not enforced at all. An admin who sets `fee_bps` expecting every deposit to leave a fee behind will see zero fees collected from any depositor who picks the fee-free entry point.

The rule `rule_reject_foreign_owned_vault` constrains `*a[0].owner != crate::ID` — i.e. the account that will be reinterpreted as the `Vault` is foreign-owned — and then asserts that `DepositContext::load` rejects it. The Prover refuted this: the trace shows `vault_info owner: '0xfffffffffffffffeb46b8ed2a85fb4a8'` versus `vault program id: '0x95d3...b4a9'`, descends into `DepositContext::load` → `DepositContext::validate`, returns with no error branch, and reports `accepted: '1'`, failing the assertion at `standard_deposits.rs:629`.

The root cause is visible directly in the source. `DepositContext::load` obtains slot 0 via `next_account_info(iter)?.try_into()?`, dispatching to:

```rust
impl<'info> TryFrom<&AccountInfo<'info>> for VaultInfo<'info> {
    fn try_from(info: &AccountInfo<'info>) -> Result<Self, Self::Error> {
        // owned by vault program
        // has discriminant
        Self { info: info.clone() }.validate()
    }
}
```

Both named checks exist only as comments; neither was implemented. `VaultInfo::validate` reduces to `self.get()?.validate()?`, where `VaultInfo::get` is `bytemuck::from_bytes::<Vault>(&data[0..size_of::<Vault>()])` — a raw reinterpretation under which every byte string of the correct length is a structurally valid `Vault`, since the type is `Pod` and carries no discriminator. `Vault::validate` is a single `require_ne!(self.assets_mint, self.shares_mint, VaultError::GuardFail)`.

The remainder of `DepositContext::validate` supplies no independent anchor. It compares `vault.assets_mint == assets_mint.key`, `vault.shares_mint == shares_mint.key` and `vault.vault_assets_account == vault_assets_account.key`, then re-derives the two PDAs via `check_vault`. Every left-hand side is read out of the same attacker-controlled bytes, so the account set is self-consistent by construction: the attacker writes their own mint and token-account keys into the fake vault and then passes exactly those accounts. It is a closed loop whose only missing external anchor is the owner check. This matches the trace, which enters `validate` and returns without erroring.

The counterexample's `num_shares == num_assets == 0` is incidental — a zero/zero pair satisfies `Vault::validate` cheaply. The defect is structural: no code on this path ever reads `info.owner`, for any vault contents. (Note also that the PDA re-derivations inside `check_vault` run against a summarized `Pubkey::create_program_address`, so the witness's PDA match is unconstrained rather than demonstrated; this neither strengthens nor weakens the finding, since the missing owner check is independent of it.)

Scope: the assertion concerns `DepositContext::load`'s return value only, so the trace proves validation accepts a foreign-owned account, not a completed exploit transaction. That this acceptance propagates to the handlers rests on reading `deposit.rs` and `deposit_exact.rs` — both call `DepositContext::load(accounts)?` first and neither consults `vault_info.owner` afterwards. `VaultInfo::try_from` is also the single construction site shared by `RedeemSharesContext`, `UpdateRewardContext` and `SlashContext`, so the same hole plausibly reaches those instructions.

Recommended fix, in `impl TryFrom<&AccountInfo<'info>> for VaultInfo<'info>` (`programs/vault/src/loaders/vault_loaders.rs`), before any `bytemuck` reinterpretation: (1) require `info.owner == &crate::ID`, returning `ProgramError::IllegalOwner`; (2) add a leading discriminator or `is_initialized` marker to the `Vault` POD layout, write it at initialization, and verify it here; (3) check `info.data_len() >= size_of::<Vault>()` (ideally `==`) before the slice, which currently panics on a short account; (4) keep `Vault::validate` but run it only after the above, so `assets_mint != shares_mint` stops masquerading as an ownership check. A secondary hardening worth raising: reject when `vault_info.key` coincides with `vault_assets_account`, `shares_mint`, `user_assets_account` or `user_shares_account`.
